# Final Experimental Design

## 0. Status and Scope
- Design only; the workspace contains only task.json; no simulator; no results.
- Simulator-agnostic; interface contract.

## 1. Interface Contract (C1–C7), mapping to questions 1–6
C1 Event toggle: pirate-invasion-style event mechanics can be fully disabled. (Q2, Q5)
C2 Threshold parameterization: the event-trigger threshold is settable at runtime without code edits; the trigger condition is evaluated deterministically per tick against the logged score. (Q2)
C3 Hierarchy assignability or measurability: either a regime/toggle that produces hierarchical vs. flat agents at t=0 (V-A), or a per-tick control/authority graph sufficient to measure per-agent hierarchy depth/size (V-B). (Q3)
C4 Timestamps of per-agent birth/death events with cause codes (natural death vs. event-linked). (Q4, Q6)
C5 Per-tick score logging (world-level and agent-level points). (Q5)
C6 Seed control/determinism: RNG stream controlled by seed; same seed + same settings → identical trace. (feasibility)
C7 Run-length knob and checkpointing. (Q4)
Optionals: exogenous event-scheduling hook (enables B2′), score cap.

Variant labels:
- Hierarchy variant: V-A (assignable → randomized), V-B (emergent/measured → quasi-experimental).
- Event arms: B0 off, B1 built-in threshold, B2 threshold shift, B2′ exogenous schedule (requires C-optional).

## 2. Estimands (before estimators)
Let each agent i in world w have survival time T_iw, censoring C; treatment arms.

Primary estimand in words: the causal contrast in the distribution of agent survival times under "event mechanics operating at the built-in threshold" vs. "event mechanics disabled," and whether this contrast differs between hierarchical and flat agents — i.e., an interaction between hierarchy and event regime in the survival distribution.

Notation: S_{a,h}(t) = P(T > t | do(event regime a), hierarchy h). Primary interaction on the RMST scale:
Δ = [RMST_{B1,H} − RMST_{B0,H}] − [RMST_{B1,F} − RMST_{B0,F}], where RMST_{a,h}(τ) = ∫₀^τ S_{a,h}(t)dt for a pre-fixed horizon τ (end of run or the 90th percentile of observed follow-up).
Secondary on the hazard scale: HR interaction θ_int in a Cox model: λ(t | a, h, X) = λ₀(t) exp(β1·a + β2·h + β3·a·h + γ'X); report θ_int = β3 with caveats (HR interaction is non-collapsible).

Score gating as post-baseline exposure: an event "occurs" at the time world score crosses the threshold; crossing time is affected by hierarchy → exposure is post-baseline and hierarchy-dependent. Therefore, a naive comparison at B1 (hierarchical worlds vs. flat worlds, both with event) is confounded: hierarchy affects both score accumulation (→ earlier crossing, more exposure) and survival via other pathways. Which arm identifies what:

- B0 (both hierarchy levels, event off): identifies the pure effect of hierarchy on survival absent events — necessary baseline-difference check; under V-A it is randomized and causal.
- B1 vs. B0 within hierarchy level h: identifies the event effect within h (under V-A randomization at the world level, arm assignment is exogenous; within-hierarchy contrasts are fine; the problem is only cross-hierarchy comparisons and the interpretation of the interaction if hierarchy is emergent).
- B2′ (exogenous schedule): breaks the score→event link; identifies "the effect of event timing/exposure as a scheduled intervention," not the effect of the natural threshold mechanic. What you gain: exposure becomes baseline-assigned; what it costs: it's a different intervention (effectiveness/pragmatic vs. explanatory gap — if agents can affect score, they can also affect natural event timing; B2′ answers "what happens if invasions occur at time t" and not "what happens if invasions occur upon reaching score").
- Conditioning on the score process: treat score as a **mediator** of the hierarchy→survival pathway (hierarchy changes score accumulation → crossing time → event exposure). Conditioning on time-varying score in a hazard model (a) blocks part of the mediated pathway — overadjustment if the total effect is the target — but (b) is necessary for certain "controlled direct effect"/within-score stratum interpretations, and for adjusting residual imbalance. Position: primary analysis estimates the **total effect of the event regime** (no conditioning on post-baseline score, except for baseline score); score-conditioned models are secondary, reported as interventional/separation-style controlled direct-effect-style contrasts, with explicit collider warning (conditioning on a mediator affected by both hierarchy and unmeasured world factors opens collider paths). Sensitivity analysis: g-computation / inverse probability of censoring weighting as needed.

## 3. Units, dependence, sample size
- Randomization unit: world (an independently seeded run). Observation unit: agent. Hierarchy under V-A is also at world/regime level (hierarchy is typically a property of the population/regime, not of individual agents — note: in some simulators hierarchy is agent-level; retain the option). Dependence: agents are nested within worlds; worlds sharing seeds across arms form pairs. Model with world-clustered robust SEs / frailty; do not treat agents as independent.
- 30–100 worlds per cell: justification without fabricated ICC: (a) cost-based upper bound — compute the number of cells k = 2 hierarchy × (2–4) arms × number of pairing strata; set N to a range from the affordable wall-clock budget; (b) precision-based: with per-world RMST estimates, SE ≈ σ_w/√N; choose N so that 95% CI half-width ≤ δ_min. δ_min is the smallest interaction effect the team considers decision-relevant, pre-specified qualitatively; σ_w is to be estimated not fabricated but from a pre-registered small pilot (e.g., 5 worlds in two corner cells) — the pilot is part of the design and is an actual estimate, not an assumption; (c) decision-theoretic sequential rule: after a pilot + first 30 worlds/cell, a pre-registered interim precision check permits addition up to the cap; stop for futility only on CI width, not on significance (avoiding bias). Further cluster considerations: under V-B (emergent), the effective number of independent hierarchy levels is small (worlds with/without emergent hierarchy are self-selected), so power for the interaction relies on within-world agent contrasts; state this.

## 4. Censoring and competing risks
- Administrative censoring at end of run (fixed tick) — independent of event process by design → KM/Cox are fine.
- Death mechanism: if invasions kill directly and also cause death indirectly via point loss → two causes. If the question is about **all-cause survival time**, competing risks are irrelevant for the primary outcome (all-cause death is a terminal event regardless of cause); cause-specific hazards are secondary and descriptive, used to check the mechanism (direct kill vs. starvation-like pathway). If "score depletion" can kill, and point theft is also the event's outcome — the event is on the causal pathway to a competing cause; since primary is all-cause, report the CIF of cause-specific deaths with competing risks (Aalen–Johansen) descriptively. Cure/non-cure not needed because τ is fixed. If immortal agents exist (a possible mechanic), stratify / treat as a separate stratum — unverifiable here.

## 5. Survivor selection into exposure — a standalone paragraph
Problem: "exposure" (an invasion has occurred in the world by time t) is only definable for worlds/agents still running and with score above threshold; agents who die early never become "exposed"; if hierarchical agents die early for other reasons, the surviving population of the exposed stratum is selected for frailty → within-exposure comparisons confounded by survivor selection; additionally, person-time of exposed agents is conditioned on having survived to the exposure time. Remedy: the primary analysis is **not** an "exposed vs. unexposed agent" analysis. It is intention-to-treat-like at the world level: an agent's "treatment" is the assigned event regime of its world from t=0 (like a target trial: eligibility = agents born at run start; treatment = arm of the world assigned at t=0; follow-up from t=0 to death or τ). Because the arm is randomized before score/death is determined, ITT avoids survivor selection into exposure. The per-tick hazard model with time-varying "event regime" is not used as primary; descriptive per-tick hazard models with time-varying "post-first-invasion" indicator are reported as secondary with explicit caveat (immortal-time bias: agents must survive to the first invasion to contribute person-time to the post-invasion period → landmines). Remedy: counting-process Cox with delayed entry at the world's first invasion time (left truncation) for a within-arm "invasion effect" analysis. And an "as-exposed" g-method (sequential g-formula) as sensitivity analysis. Falsification criterion for the interaction claim: require (i) a CI for the RMST interaction Δ excluding a pre-specified null band computed from paired-seed permutation, (ii) the same interaction pattern in the B2′ exogenous arm (not dependent on natural exposure), (iii) no interaction in the B0 vs. B0′ sham contrast (a pseudo-arm differing only in logging/irrelevant parameters — negative control). The interaction is rejected if driven by differential survival to the threshold: check by re-estimating Δ restricted to agents that survive to the pre-specified early time τ0 (before any invasion) in both arms (a landmark at a common pre-exposure time, with proper risk-set handling). Also, report the distribution of invasion times by hierarchy arm; if hierarchical worlds cross significantly earlier, then the natural-threshold interaction under B1 conflates timing and susceptibility — B2′ is the disambiguator.

## 6. Analysis plan (estimators)
- Primary: RMST_{a,h}(τ) via Kaplan–Meier per cell (aggregation across worlds by pooling at the world level? — better: weighted average of world-level KM, or a frailty model); interaction Δ and 95% CI from a pseudo-value / bootstrap clustered at the world level (resample worlds).
- Cox shared frailty (gamma), cluster=world, covariates arm, hierarchy, arm×hierarchy + baseline covariates only; Efron; PH check; AFT Weibull as sensitivity analysis.
- Paired-seed analysis: worlds sharing seeds are paired across arms; difference-in-differences of world-level RMST, with permutation tests across seed pairs.
- Secondary: cause-specific hazards/Aalen-Johansen; delayed-entry Cox for within-arm invasion effect; sequential g-formula conditioning on the time-varying score as a controlled direct-effect-style estimate (with collider caveats); leader vs. follower stratification under V-A.
- Multiple comparisons: pre-register one primary contrast family (Δ), everything else secondary/exploratory.
- Pre-registration: register protocol + analysis code before compute; report TRIPOD/TST-style target-trial elements.

## 7. Pre-registered feasibility checks (with degradation)
F1 Does the threshold parameter exist / is the event togglable? Fail → arms collapse to on/off or require code edits (patch review; risk of behavior change; downgrade the threshold arm).
F2 Determinism given seed (run same seed twice → identical trace). Fail → pair analyses and paired permutation tests invalidated; larger N, world-level randomization checks.
F3 Is per-tick score logging cheap? Fail (too slow) → log at checkpoints (e.g., every 100 ticks) or reconstruct from per-agent points; degrade score as time-varying covariate to piecewise; mediator analysis coarsened.
F4 Wall-clock per world × number of cells vs. budget → set N via decision-theoretic rule; fail → reduce B2 shift level or number of B2′ schedule variants (keep 2×2 core).
F5 Is hierarchy assignable? No → V-B quasi-experimental branch; downgrade interaction claims to association; add matched hierarchy-depth strata.
F6 Existence of exogenous scheduling hook? No → B2′ requires code injection into event scheduler; if unacceptable → drop B2′; identification of timing vs. susceptibility relies only on score-conditioned sensitivity analyses; the main weakness remains open.
F7 Birth/death event log with cause codes; if absent → infer from state diffs; possible outcome misclassification; validate on manual inspection of a few runs.
F8 Are agents replaceable/respawning? Define t=0 eligibility accordingly.

## 8. Unverifiable register
- Identity of the codebase (aevol is a hypothesis), threshold values, direction/comparison of the trigger, death rules, immortals, respawn, whether hierarchy is a regime setting or emergent, whether events kill directly, score semantics — none observed; nothing in this section is based on any executed code; no numbers in this document other than generic design parameters (τ, τ0, N range) are claimed to be simulator-specific.

Controls: identical seeds/streams; same initial population composition where the simulator allows; single-factor parameter change per arm; fixed run length; matched logging overhead across arms (sham arm B0′ for negative control); pre-registered contrast set.

